# vLLM's rows: one a model the server can serve, each the model and its server flags, named by
# the pin (VLLM_ROWS in vllm.env). The pin names each flavor's default row, VLLM_MODEL_CUDA's on
# the GB10s and VLLM_MODEL_ROCM's on the Framework; `VLLM_MODEL=<row id> host/engines vllm update`
# serves another for one run (docs/engines/vllm/models.md, "Changing the model"). The rows are one
# set for both flavors, though not every row serves on both: NVFP4 is CUDA's alone.
#
# A row is its id in brackets, then the model's Hugging Face repository, then its server flags,
# over as many lines as read well. vllm.sh puts --host and --port after the model, so a row never
# names them. An id is the repository's name without its organization, with a suffix for a second
# row of the same model; each is unique. No flag or value holds a space or a #, since the served
# command is compared joined with spaces and # starts a comment.
#
# Every row reserves 0.80 of the GPU's memory, which leaves the rest to the host it shares it
# with, and serves sequences of 8192 tokens, as each of llama.cpp's slots. The model's own
# generation config sets its sampling (vLLM's default, --generation-config auto). Gemma 4's rows
# hold 4 sequences, mirroring llama.cpp's 4 slots; keep a BF16 KV cache, since the NVFP4
# checkpoints declare a static FP8 one but ship no calibrated k/v scales, so vLLM would quantize
# it with a scale of 1.0, and every Gemma 4 row matches them; and use Gemma 4's own reasoning and
# tool-call parsers. Their vision encoder loads (no --language-model-only), and E4B's and 12B's
# audio encoder too: vLLM allows every modality the model has without --limit-mm-per-prompt.

# The cuda flavor's default: Gemma 4 26B-A4B, MoE, NVIDIA's NVFP4. The anchors A' and B', and
# vision.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# The FlashInfer diagnostic: the pin's row on FlashInfer attention, text-only. Without a backend
# named, vLLM forces Triton for Gemma 4's mixed head sizes on the GB10, where FA4 isn't available;
# --language-model-only leaves the vision encoder out, so only the text model's attention runs.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4-flashinfer]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4
--attention-backend FLASHINFER --language-model-only

# The Marlin MoE diagnostic: the pin's row on Marlin's kernels for the NVFP4 experts. Without a
# backend named, vLLM v0.32.0 chooses FLASHINFER_CUTLASS for them on the GB10's SM121, out of the
# backends its journal lists, MARLIN among them; Marlin is the fallback in vLLM's own list, a
# weight-only kernel that runs the activations in 16 bits. The row asks whether the MoE kernel
# explains vLLM's slow NVFP4 decode, so only the MoE backend changes.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4-marlin]
nvidia/Gemma-4-26B-A4B-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4
--moe-backend marlin

# Gemma 4 26B-A4B, MoE, Google's BF16, with its vision encoder: the MoE shape in a format ROCm
# serves, where the NVFP4 rows are CUDA's alone. The anchors and vision on the Framework.
[gemma-4-26B-A4B-it]
google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 E4B, dense small, BF16.
[gemma-4-E4B-it]
google/gemma-4-E4B-it
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 E4B, Google's quantization-aware trained w4a16: the quantization pair beside BF16.
[gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-w4a16-ct]
google/gemma-4-E4B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 12B, dense mid, Google's QAT w4a16.
[gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct]
google/gemma-4-12B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 31B, dense large, NVIDIA's NVFP4; it declares the static FP8 KV cache too.
[Gemma-4-31B-IT-NVFP4]
nvidia/Gemma-4-31B-IT-NVFP4
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# Gemma 4 31B, dense large, Google's QAT w4a16: the dense large shape in a format ROCm serves.
[gemma-4-31B-it-qat-w4a16-ct]
google/gemma-4-31B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
--max-num-seqs 4 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--kv-cache-dtype bfloat16
--reasoning-parser gemma4 --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser gemma4

# IBM's granite-embedding-english-r2, BF16, served for /v1/embeddings only: vLLM's pooling runner,
# which its architecture selects anyway, named so the row says what it serves. A ModernBERT
# encoder with heads of 64, whose encoder-only attention runs on FLASH_ATTN on the GB10's SM121.
# 16 sequences, one a stream of the embedding cell's 16. No parsers: it generates nothing.
[granite-embedding-english-r2]
ibm-granite/granite-embedding-english-r2
--runner pooling
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80

# OpenAI's gpt-oss-120b in its native MXFP4, the rocm flavor's default, at 16 sequences,
# mirroring llama.cpp's 16-slot section: its anchor-load cells and the concurrency sweep run on
# this one start. Its KV cache stays the checkpoint's (BF16), and its parsers are gpt-oss's:
# openai_gptoss for the reasoning, which vLLM would choose for gpt-oss anyway, and openai for the
# tool calls.
[gpt-oss-120b]
openai/gpt-oss-120b
--max-num-seqs 16 --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.80
--reasoning-parser openai_gptoss --enable-auto-tool-choice --tool-call-parser openai
